#align(center)[#text(size: 16pt, weight: "bold")[Fiber functors for finite symmetric tensor categories \
in positive characteristic]]
#align(center)[OpenAI]
#align(center)[September 24, 2026]

== 1 Introduction <sec:introduction>

Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p > 0$.
  A #emph[finite symmetric tensor category over $k$] is an essentially small $k$-linear abelian rigid symmetric
  monoidal category with biexact tensor product, finite-dimensional morphism spaces, finite-length objects,
  finitely many isomorphism classes of simple objects, enough projectives, and $"End" \( upright(bold(1)) \) = k$.
  A #emph[fiber functor] between such categories is a $k$-linear exact faithful strong symmetric monoidal functor.
  The higher Verlinde categories of Benson–Etingof–Ostrik form a tower

$ "Ver"_p \( k \) subset "Ver"_(p^2) \( k \) subset "Ver"_(p^3) \( k \) subset dots.h.c $

of finite symmetric tensor categories #cite(label("BEO2023")) #cite(label("Coulembier2021Envelopes")).
  The first level is the semisimplification of $"Rep"_k \( bb(Z) \/ p \)$:
  one quotients by morphisms $f : X arrow.r Y$ satisfying $"tr" \( g f \) = 0$ for all $g : Y arrow.r X$.
  Its simple objects are represented by Jordan blocks of lengths $1 \, dots.h \, p -
  1$ #cite(label("EtingofOstrikFrobenius")) Section 2.3. Thus $"Ver"_2$ is the category of vector spaces and
  $"Ver"_3$ is the category of supervector spaces with its sign symmetry.
  The higher levels need not be semisimple. They are finite abelian envelopes of quotients of the category of
  tilting $S L_2$-modules (modules with both Weyl and dual Weyl filtrations),
  where the tensor ideal generated by the Steinberg module of highest weight $p^n -
  1$ is killed at level $n$ #cite(label("BEO2023")) Sections 3.1 and 4.1, Theorem 4.2. Our main result is the following.

#thm([Theorem 1.1.])[
  #emph[For every prime $p$, every algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $p$,
    and every finite symmetric tensor category $cal(C)$ over $k$, there are an integer $n gt.eq 1$ and a fiber functor
$ cal(C) arrow.r "Ver"_(p^n) \( k \) . $

]
] <thm:main>

This proves the finite case of the Benson–Etingof–Ostrik conjecture #cite(label("BEO2023")) Conjecture 1.4,
  including characteristic two. The full conjecture concerns symmetric tensor categories of #emph[moderate
  growth]: for each object, the lengths of its tensor powers grow at most exponentially.
  Its target is the union of the higher Verlinde tower. Here both the projective generator and the finite set of
  simple classes are essential. The theorem places the image in a single finite level,
  whose height may depend on $cal(C)$; it makes no assertion about arbitrary nonfinite categories of moderate growth.

A finite symmetric tensor category is #emph[incompressible] if every $k$-linear exact symmetric tensor functor out
  of it is fully faithful. Theorem 1 and the higher Verlinde subcategory classification of Benson–Etingof–Ostrik
  #cite(label("BEO2023")) classify these finite categories,
  giving the finite case of Coulembier–Etingof–Ostrik’s Conjecture B
  #cite(label("CoulembierEtingofOstrikIncompressible")). In characteristic two,
  their subterminality theorem further gives uniqueness up to tensor isomorphism of the functor into the tower
  union. Their generalized Tannaka theorem also realizes every finite category as representations of an affine
  group scheme internal to a chosen finite level, with the canonical fundamental-group compatibility.
  Section 8 gives the precise statements and deductions.

=== Background and contribution <background-and-contribution>

In characteristic zero, Deligne’s theorem makes supervector spaces a common target for fiber functors from
  symmetric tensor categories of moderate growth #cite(label("Deligne2002")).
  In positive characteristic, Ostrik proved that symmetric fusion categories admit fiber functors to $"Ver"_p$
  #cite(label("Ostrik2020")) Theorem 1.5. Etingof–Ostrik constructed the ordinary Frobenius functor for
  nonsemisimple categories; Coulembier independently constructed its underlying additive functor as the external
  Frobenius twist #cite(label("Coulembier2020Tannakian")) Section 4.3.
  Etingof–Ostrik established its symmetric monoidal structure and characterized the finite categories on which it
  is exact by the existence of a fiber functor to $"Ver"_p$ #cite(label("EtingofOstrikFrobenius")) Proposition 5.1
  and Theorem 8.1. Coulembier–Etingof–Ostrik extended that characterization to categories of moderate growth
  #cite(label("CoulembierEtingofOstrik2023")).

The higher levels are needed because the ordinary Frobenius functor need not be exact.
  The characteristic-two examples of Benson–Etingof #cite(label("BensonEtingof2019")) led to the all-prime tower
  of Benson–Etingof–Ostrik #cite(label("BEO2023")), constructed independently through abelian envelopes by
  Coulembier #cite(label("Coulembier2021Envelopes")). Coulembier–Flake developed higher Frobenius functors from
  elementary abelian group representations #cite(label("CoulembierFlakeHigher")).
  Their construction becomes symmetric monoidal once a specific restricted-tilting conjecture is established.
  Besides the rank-one case, the conjecture was already known for $p = r =
  2$ #cite(label("CoulembierFlakeHigher")) Remark 5.4.4(1) and Section 6.
  Their subsequent work proved that assertion for substantial classes of modules,
  including modules of Loewy length at most two and Carlson modules,
  and for the subcategory they generate under tensor products, duals, syzygy shifts, finite sums,
  and summands #cite(label("CoulembierFlakeSteinberg")) Theorem 2.
  For rank two they also established the assertion for every cyclic summand of the relevant Steinberg tensor
  product #cite(label("CoulembierFlakeSteinberg")) Theorem 3.

Our independent representation-theoretic contribution is the full restricted-tilting conjecture,
  proved in Theorem 4. For $r gt.eq 1$, embed $E_r = \( bb(Z) \/ p \)^r$ in the upper unipotent subgroup of $S
  L_2 \( k \)$, and write $R$ for restriction along this embedding.
  Let $"St"_(r - 1)$ be the simple $S L_2$-module of highest weight $p^(r - 1) - 1$.
  For every finite-dimensional $E_r$-module $D$, we show that

$ R \( "St"_(r - 1) \) ⊗ D tilde.equiv R \( T \) $

for a tilting $S L_2$-module $T$. This is #cite(label("CoulembierFlakeHigher")) Conjecture 5.4.2(1),
  also formulated as #cite(label("CoulembierFlakeSteinberg")) Conjecture 1.
  The proof lifts $D$ to the additive group, extends the associated equivariant vector bundle from the punctured
  plane across the origin, and uses a formal comparison of fibers after tensoring by the Steinberg module.
  The bounded-weight tilting input is the established #cite(label("BEO2023")) Lemma 3.3,
  in the extension-closed form also used in #cite(label("CoulembierFlakeSteinberg")) proof of Lemma 3.1.6.
  The geometric comparison is the additional step that treats arbitrary $D$.

=== Proof strategy <proof-strategy>

The restricted-tilting theorem supplies the hypothesis for the Coulembier–Flake construction.
  For each embedding of $E_r$ as above, Section 4 therefore produces an additive strong symmetric monoidal functor

$ Phi : cal(C) arrow.r cal(C) times.square "Ver"_(p^r) \( k \) . $

Here $times.square$ denotes the Deligne tensor product of finite abelian categories.
  The functor is initially semilinear: $Phi \( a f \) = a^(p^r) Phi \( f \)$ for $a in k$ and a morphism $f$.
  Its exactness depends on the embedding.

To select an exact functor, choose a projective generator $P$ of $cal(C)$.
  The regular permutation action of $E_r$ on $p^r$ letters makes $"Hom" \( P \, P^(⊗ p^r) \)$ an $E_r$-module.
  Section 5 proves that this module is nonprojective for some $r$,
  using projectivity detection and the symmetrizer criterion of #cite(label("CoulembierEtingofOstrik2023"))
  Lemma 4.10. At the least such $r$, rank support selects an embedding for which $Phi$ is exact (Theorem 18 in
  Section 6).

It remains to remove the $cal(C)$ factor. Section 7 compares the Frobenius–Perron dimensions on that side of
  $Phi$: if $S$ is simple and $A times.square B$ is a simple constituent of $Phi \( S \)$,
  then the dimension of $A$ is at most that of $S$. Equality forces $S$ into the Frobenius-exact subcategory of
  $cal(C)$ and makes $A$ the only possible first component.
  In the finite graph with arrows $S arrow.r A$, every cycle therefore lies in that subcategory and cannot be
  left. After finitely many iterations, all first components lie there,
  so the image lies in its Serre closure on the first side.
  This adapts the finite-simple descent of #cite(label("EtingofOstrikFrobenius")) Section 10 to the exact higher
  functor. The Serre-closure results of that paper provide a fiber functor to $"Ver"_p$ in odd characteristic and
  to $"Ver"_4^(+) subset "Ver"_4$ in characteristic two. Combining the resulting factors in one tower level and
  correcting the scalar semilinearity completes Theorem 1.

== 2 Finite tensor categories and products <sec:preliminaries>

Throughout, $k$ is algebraically closed of characteristic $p > 0$.
  Unless explicitly stated otherwise, group representations and rational algebraic-group modules are finite
  dimensional over $k$. A tensor functor is strong monoidal;
  when called symmetric it also preserves the given symmetries.
  We first relate the hypotheses of Theorem 1 to the usual finite-tensor-category conventions.

=== 2.1 The finite-category conventions <the-finite-category-conventions>

#thm([Lemma 2.1.])[
  #emph[Under the conventions of Theorem 1, the unit of $cal(C)$ is simple and $cal(C)$ has a projective generator
    $P$. The functor $omega = "Hom"_(cal(C)) \( P \, - \)$ is exact and faithful,
    and $cal(C)$ is equivalent as a $k$-linear abelian category to the category of finite-dimensional modules over
    a finite-dimensional $k$-algebra.]
] <lem:finite-conventions>

#proof[
  We recall the unit argument of Deligne–Milne #cite(label("DeligneMilne1982")) Proposition 1.17.
    Let $i : U arrow.r.hook upright(bold(1))$ and $V = "coker" i$.
    The epimorphism $V ⊗ U arrow.l U$ induced by $upright(bold(1)) arrow.r.twohead
    V$ has zero composite with the monomorphism $V ⊗ i : V ⊗ U arrow.r V$. Thus $V ⊗ U = 0$.
    Put $W = ker \( i^(*) : upright(bold(1)) arrow.r U^(*) \)$.
    For a subobject $j : T arrow.r.hook X$, adjunction and exactness give
  $ T ⊗ U = 0 quad arrow.l.r.double quad \( i^(*) ⊗ "id"_X \) j : T arrow.r U^(*) ⊗ X upright(" is zero") . $
  Consequently $W ⊗ X$ is the largest subobject of $X$ annihilated by tensoring with $U$.
    Taking $X = upright(bold(1)) \, V$ gives $W ⊗ U = 0$ and $W ⊗ V tilde.eq V$.
    Tensoring $0 arrow.r U arrow.r upright(bold(1)) arrow.r V arrow.r
    0$ with $W$ now shows that the natural map $W arrow.r V$ is an isomorphism.
    Hence $upright(bold(1)) = U xor W$. Since $"End" \( upright(bold(1)) \) = k$ has no nontrivial idempotents,
    $upright(bold(1))$ is simple.
  Choose representatives $S_1 \, dots.h \, S_t$ of the simple objects and projective objects $Q_i$ with
    epimorphisms $Q_i arrow.r.twohead S_i$. Set $P = xor.big_i Q_i$.
    Induction on length, using projectivity to lift an epimorphism onto a simple quotient,
    shows that every object is a quotient of a finite direct sum of copies of $P$.
    Thus $P$ is a projective generator. Evaluation by $P$ is exact;
    it is faithful because a nonzero image of a morphism receives a nonzero map from $P$,
    which lifts across the epimorphism onto that image. Writing $A = "End" \( P \)^(upright("op"))$,
    finite presentations by copies of $P$ identify $cal(C)$ with $A$-modules of finite dimension.
    Indeed, evaluation is fully faithful on such presentations,
    and every finite $A$-module has a presentation by finite free $A$-modules.
    The algebra $A$ is finite-dimensional by the Hom-space hypothesis. ◻
]

We fix such a $P$. In particular there is an epimorphism $P arrow.r.twohead upright(bold(1))$:
  the unit is one of the $S_i$. The finite-algebra description also supplies projective covers of all simple
  objects whenever they are needed below.

=== 2.2 Deligne products <deligne-products>

For finite $k$-linear abelian categories $cal(A)$ and $cal(B)$,
  their Deligne product $cal(A) times.square cal(B)$ represents $k$-bilinear functors that are right exact in each
  variable. If $cal(A) tilde.eq A upright("-") upright("mod")$ and $cal(B) tilde.eq B upright("-")
  upright("mod")$, then $cal(A) times.square cal(B) tilde.eq \( A ⊗_k B \) upright("-") upright("mod")$.
  Since $k$ is algebraically closed, its simple objects are the exterior products of simple objects of the two
  categories. Deligne products of finite tensor categories carry the componentwise tensor structure.

#thm([Lemma 2.2.])[
  #emph[Let $cal(A)_1 \, dots.h \, cal(A)_s \, cal(Z)$ be finite symmetric tensor categories.
    Exact $k$-linear symmetric tensor functors $F_i : cal(A)_i arrow.r
    cal(Z)$ induce an exact $k$-linear symmetric tensor functor
$ H : cal(A)_1 times.square dots.h.c times.square cal(A)_s arrow.r cal(Z) \, wide
  H \( X_1 times.square dots.h.c times.square X_s \) = ⨂_i F_i \( X_i \) . $

 Deligne products of exact tensor functors are exact as well.]
] <lem:combine-functors>

#proof[
  The displayed multilinear functor extends to a right-exact functor by the universal property.
    Symmetry in $cal(Z)$ supplies the tensor constraint by reordering the factors.
    The constraint, its inverse, and the coherence identities extend from exterior products by that same property.
    The resulting strong monoidal functor preserves duals. Dualizing a short exact sequence,
    applying right exactness, and dualizing back proves left exactness.
    The identical argument, with the componentwise tensor structure, proves the last assertion. ◻
]

== 3 Restricted tilting modules <sec:restricted>

Let $G = upright("SL")_(2 \, k)$, let $V = k^2$ be its standard module, and set

$ u \( t \) = mat(delim: "(", 1, t; 0, 1) . $

Let $U tilde.eq bb(G)_a$ be the upper unitriangular subgroup, parameterized by these matrices.
  Write $L \( h \)$ for the simple rational $G$-module of highest weight $h gt.eq 0$, and $"St"_m =
  L \( p^m - 1 \)$ for the $m$th Steinberg module; in particular $"St"_0 = k$.
  The costandard and Weyl modules are $nabla \( a \) = "Sym"^a \( V^(*) \)$ and $Delta \( a \) =
  nabla \( a \)^(*)$. Here $V tilde.eq V^(*)$ by the invariant determinant pairing.
  A module is #emph[tilting] if it has both a filtration by costandard modules and a filtration by Weyl modules.
  Denote by $T \( a \)$ the indecomposable tilting module of highest weight $a$.

#thm([Theorem 3.1.])[
  #emph[Let $r gt.eq 1$, let $E_r = \( bb(Z) \/ p \)^r$, and let $iota :
    E_r arrow.r.hook U \( k \)$ be any embedding. Write $R_iota$ for restriction along $iota$.
    For every finite-dimensional $k E_r$-module $D$, there is a tilting $G$-module $T$ such that
$ R_iota \( "St"_(r - 1) \) ⊗ D tilde.eq R_iota \( T \) . $

]
] <thm:restricted-tilting>

This proves the restricted-Steinberg assertion of #cite(label("CoulembierFlakeSteinberg")) Conjecture 1,
  for every embedding and every prime. The proof has three steps.
  We lift $D$ to a vector bundle on the plane with bounded highest weights at the origin.
  Tensoring with $"St"_(r - 1)$ makes that origin fibre tilting.
  Finally, an equivariant formal comparison identifies its restriction to $U$ with the fibre on the punctured
  plane. Write $R = R_iota$ within this section.

=== 3.1 A vector bundle with bounded origin weights <subsec:geometric-lift>

For a finite-dimensional rational $U$-module $D$, its #emph[degree] is the largest degree of an entry of its
  action matrix $rho_D \( u \( t \) \)$. This number is independent of the chosen basis.
  The zero module will always satisfy every degree bound below.

Keep the embedding $iota : E_r arrow.r.hook U \( k \)$ fixed, and put $q = p^r$.
  We will replace an arbitrary $k E_r$-module by a $G$-module at the origin of the standard plane.
  The two modules need not be isomorphic on restriction; their comparison will come after tensoring with a
  Steinberg module.

#thm([Proposition 3.2.])[
  #emph[For every finite-dimensional $k E_r$-module $D$, there is a $G$-equivariant vector bundle $cal(M)$ on
    $bb(A)_k^2$ such that the stabilizer action on the fibre at $e_1 = \( 1 \, 0 \)$ restricts to $D$ along
    $iota$. Every simple composition factor of the rational $G$-module $M_0 =
    cal(M) \|_0$ has highest weight less than $q$.]
] <prop:bounded-origin>

#proof[
  We first lift $D$ to a rational $U$-module of degree less than $q$.
    Let $H subset k$ be the image of $E_r$ under its additive embedding. The vector space
  $ C_q = k \[ t \]_(< q) = "span"_k { 1 \, t \, dots.h \, t^(q - 1) } $
  is a subcoalgebra of $k \[ t \]$, with comultiplication $Delta f \( t \, s \) =
    f \( t + s \)$ and counit $f mapsto f \( 0 \)$. Evaluation induces a coalgebra map
  $ "ev"_H : C_q arrow.r k^H = cal(O) \( E_r \) . $
  It is bijective: a polynomial of degree less than $q$ vanishing at the $q$ distinct elements of $H$ is zero,
    and both spaces have dimension $q$. Transporting the $cal(O) \( E_r \)$-coaction of $D$ across this
    isomorphism and then including $C_q subset k \[ t \]$ gives a rational $U$-module $hat(D)$ of degree less than
    $q$. Its restriction is $D$. This is a lift of individual representations;
    we do not require the lifting operation to preserve tensor products.
  The orbit map $G arrow.r bb(A)^2 \\ { 0 }$, $g mapsto g e_1$, is a right $U$-torsor.
    Indeed its fibres are right $U$-cosets, and on the opens $x eq.not 0$ and $y eq.not
    0$ it has the respective sections
  $ mat(delim: "(", x, 0; y, x^(- 1)) \, wide mat(delim: "(", x, - y^(- 1); y, 0) . $
  Form the associated vector bundle
  $ cal(E) = G times^U hat(D) \, wide \( g \, d \) tilde.op \( g u \, u^(- 1) d \) \, $
  on this punctured plane, with $G$ acting by left multiplication.
    The fibre identification $d mapsto \[ 1 \, d \]$ at $e_1$ gives precisely the prescribed $U$-action on $hat(D)$.
  We next extend the bundle and its equivariance across the origin.
    A coherent extension $cal(F)$ of $cal(E)$ exists on the noetherian affine plane.
    Its double dual $cal(M) = cal(F)^(* *)$ is coherent and reflexive, and still restricts to $cal(E)$.
    On a regular surface a reflexive coherent sheaf is locally free:
    its stalk has depth two at a closed point, so the Auslander–Buchsbaum formula gives projective dimension zero;
    the stalks of dimensions zero and one are free as well. These standard extension statements can be found in
    #cite(label("StacksProject")) Tags 0G41, 0AY4, and 0B3N.
  Write $a : G times bb(A)^2 arrow.r bb(A)^2$ for the action and $pi_2$ for the projection.
    Over the punctured plane the bundle action is an isomorphism $pi_2^(*) cal(M) arrow.r a^(*) cal(M)$.
    Both sheaves are vector bundles on the regular variety $G times bb(A)^2$.
    Their homomorphism sheaf is locally free, and its sections extend uniquely across $G times { 0 }$,
    which has codimension two. This is the usual extension property for reflexive sheaves
    #cite(label("StacksProject")) Tag 0EBJ. Extend the isomorphism and its inverse;
    their composites are the identity by uniqueness. The identity and cocycle conditions hold on the punctured
    plane and therefore, by the same uniqueness on $bb(A)^2$ and $G times G times bb(A)^2$, hold everywhere.
    Thus $cal(M)$ is a $G$-equivariant vector bundle.
  It remains to bound the highest weights of $M_0$. The line $ell = k e_1$ is fixed pointwise by $U$.
    For $a in k^times$ put $h_a = "diag" \( a \, a^(- 1) \)$.
    The bundle action of $h_a$ identifies the fibres at $e_1$ and $a e_1$, and
  $ h_a^(- 1) u \( t \) h_a = u \( a^(- 2) t \) . $
  Consequently the $U$-action on every nonzero fibre of $cal(M) \|_ell$ has degree less than $q$.
  To pass this bound to the origin, let $A = Gamma \( ell \, cal(E) n d \( cal(M) \|_ell \) \)$.
    Since $U$ acts trivially on the base $ell$, its bundle action is an element of $A ⊗_k k \[ t \]$,
    say $sum_(j = 0)^N b_j t^j$. For $j gt.eq q$, the section $b_j$ vanishes on every nonzero fibre.
    The field $k$ is infinite and $cal(E) n d \( cal(M) \|_ell \)$ is locally free, so $b_j = 0$.
    Specialization at zero therefore gives an action on $M_0$ of degree less than $q$.
    This bound passes to submodules and quotients by writing the action in a basis adapted to a submodule.
  Finally, if $L \( h \)$ is the simple $G$-module of highest weight $h$, its $U$-action has degree exactly $h$.
    To see this, write $h = sum_i h_i p^i$ with $0 lt.eq h_i < p$.
    Steinberg’s tensor product theorem, in the form recalled in #cite(label("DotyHenke2005")) Lemma 1.1 and the
    proof of Theorem 2.1, gives
  $ L \( h \) tilde.equiv ⨂_i #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] "Sym"^(h_i) \( k^2 \)
    #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]^(\( i \)) . $
  Here $\( i \)$ denotes Frobenius twist. The action on the $i$th factor has degree $h_i p^i$.
    On the tensor product, the coefficient from $⨂_i e_2^(h_i)$ to $⨂_i e_1^(h_i)$ is $t^h$,
    so the upper bound $h$ is attained. Hence every simple factor $L \( h \)$ of $M_0$ satisfies $h < q$,
    as required. The argument applies unchanged when $p = 2$. ◻
]

=== 3.2 The origin fibre after tensoring with a Steinberg module <subsec:bounded-tilting>

We next convert the highest-weight bound on the origin fibre into a tilting module.
  We use the theory of tilting modules developed by Donkin #cite(label("Donkin1993")).
  Tilting modules are closed under extensions and tensor products.
  For $0 lt.eq a < p$ one has $L \( a \) = T \( a \)$, and Donkin’s formula is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.1)")
 $ T \( a \) ⊗ T \( b \)^(\( 1 \)) tilde.eq T \( a + p b \)
   wide \( p - 1 lt.eq a lt.eq 2 p - 2 \, quad b gt.eq 0 \) \, $ <eq:donkin-sl2>]

where $\( 1 \)$ means pullback along the Frobenius morphism of $G$.
  The identity $L \( a \) = T \( a \)$ in the stated range and Formula (3.1) hold in every positive
  characteristic, including $2$; see #cite(label("BEO2023")) Proposition 3.1.

The following is the extension-closed form of #cite(label("BEO2023")) Lemma 3.3,
  also used in the proof of #cite(label("CoulembierFlakeSteinberg")) Lemma 3.1.6.
  We recall its short proof to make the highest-weight bound explicit.

#thm([Lemma 3.3.])[
  #emph[Let $k$ be algebraically closed of characteristic $p > 0$, let $m gt.eq 0$,
    and let $M$ be a finite-dimensional rational $S L_2 \( k \)$-module.
    If every simple composition factor of $M$ is $L \( h \)$ with $0 lt.eq h < p^(m + 1)$,
    then $"St"_m ⊗ M$ is tilting. Here $"St"_0 = k$.]
] <lem:bounded-tilting>

#proof[
  Tensoring a composition series of $M$ by $"St"_m$ and splicing Weyl and costandard filtrations reduces the
    assertion to $M = L \( h \)$. We induct on $m$. When $m = 0$,
    the inequality $h < p$ gives $"St"_0 ⊗ L \( h \) = T \( h \)$.
    For $m > 0$, write $h = d + p h'$ with $0 lt.eq d < p$ and $h' < p^m$. Steinberg’s tensor product theorem gives
  $ "St"_m ⊗ L \( h \) tilde.eq L \( d \) ⊗ "St"_1 ⊗ #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] "St"_(m - 1) ⊗ L \( h' \)
    #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]^(\( 1 \)) . $
  By induction, the module in parentheses is a direct sum of tilting modules $T \( b \)$.
    Formula (3.1), applied with $a = p - 1$, shows that tensoring its Frobenius twist with $"St"_1$ is tilting.
    Tensoring once more with $L \( d \) = T \( d \)$ preserves that property. ◻
]

Apply Lemma 6 to the origin fibre $M_0$ with $m = r - 1$. Its established bound $h < p^r$ shows that

$ T := "St"_(r - 1) ⊗ M_0 $

is tilting. This includes $r = 1$, when the Steinberg factor is trivial.
  It remains to compare this origin fibre with the given module on the punctured plane.

=== 3.3 Comparing the origin and the unipotent fibre <subsec:formal-comparison>

The bundle $cal(N) = "St"_(r - 1) ⊗_k cal(M)$ has the tilting origin fibre $T$ constructed above.
  We now recover its representation on the punctured orbit from that fibre.
  The following lemma applies to any equivariant bundle with tilting origin fibre:
  we first trivialize it equivariantly on successive infinitesimal neighbourhoods of $0$,
  then compare the generic point of the $U$-fixed line with $e_1$.

#thm([Lemma 3.4.])[
  (Comparison with a tilting origin fibre).  #emph[Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field of arbitrary positive
    characteristic. Let $cal(N)$ be a $G$-equivariant vector bundle on $V$, and suppose that its origin fibre $T =
    cal(N)_0$ is a tilting $G$-module. Then
$ cal(N)_(e_1) tilde.equiv T \|_U $

 as rational $U$-modules over $k$.]
] <lem:formal-comparison>

#proof[
  We first construct compatible equivariant trivializations on every infinitesimal neighbourhood of $0$.
    Let $A = k \[ x \, y \]$, let $frak(m) = \( x \, y \)$, and let $N = Gamma \( V \, cal(N) \)$,
    a possibly infinite-dimensional rational $G$-module. Its quotients $N \/ frak(m)^j N$ are finite dimensional.
    Local freeness gives canonical $G$-isomorphisms
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.2)")
   $ frak(m)^j N \/ frak(m)^(j + 1) N tilde.equiv \( frak(m)^j \/ frak(m)^(j + 1) \) ⊗_k T tilde.equiv
     "Sym"^j \( V^(*) \) ⊗_k T wide \( j gt.eq 0 \) . $ <eq:jet-kernel>]
  Recall that a good filtration has costandard successive quotients,
    and a Weyl filtration has standard successive quotients. For $G = upright("SL")_2$,
    the costandard module of highest weight $a$ is $nabla \( a \) = "Sym"^a \( V^(*) \)$.
    Tensor products of two such modules have good filtrations.
    Indeed, for $a \, b gt.eq 1$, multiplication of polynomials gives the exact sequence
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.3)")
   $ 0 arrow.r "Sym"^(a - 1) \( V^(*) \) ⊗ "Sym"^(b - 1) \( V^(*) \) arrow.r^(thick d thick) "Sym"^a \( V^(*) \) ⊗
     "Sym"^b \( V^(*) \) arrow.r "Sym"^(a + b) \( V^(*) \) arrow.r 0 . $ <eq:symmetric-power-filtration>]
  Here $d = x_1 y_2 - y_1 x_2$ is the determinant in two copies of $V^(*)$.
    Multiplication by $d$ is injective, its image lies in the kernel, and dimensions prove exactness.
    Induction on $min \( a \, b \)$, starting with a trivial factor, gives the assertion.
    Refining filtrations then proves the same tensor-product assertion for arbitrary modules with good
    filtrations. This argument is unchanged in characteristic two.
  The module $T$ has both a Weyl filtration and a good filtration.
    Consequently, the target of the next Ext group has a good filtration,
    and the standard–costandard Ext vanishing theorem of Cline–Parshall–Scott–van der Kallen gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.4)")
   $ "Ext"_G^1 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] T \, "Sym"^j \( V^(*) \) ⊗ T #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] =
     0 wide \( j gt.eq 0 \) . $ <eq:formal-ext-vanishing>]
  We use Ext in the category of rational $G$-modules; the vanishing follows from
    $"Ext"_G^1 \( Delta \( a \) \, nabla \( b \) \) = 0$ by induction on the two filtrations;
    see #cite(label("Riche")) Chapter 1, Proposition 2.6, and Chapter 4, Proposition 1.1.
  Starting with the identity $s_1 : T arrow.r N \/ frak(m) N$, lift successively to $G$-maps
  $ s_j : T arrow.r N \/ frak(m)^j N wide \( j gt.eq 1 \) $
  such that $s_(j + 1)$ reduces to $s_j$. Pull back the short exact sequence
  $ 0 arrow.r "Sym"^j \( V^(*) \) ⊗_k T arrow.r N \/ frak(m)^(j + 1) N arrow.r N \/ frak(m)^j N arrow.r 0 $
  along $s_j$. By (3.4) the resulting extension of $T$ splits equivariantly;
    a splitting gives the lift $s_(j + 1)$. Extending $s_j$ by scalars gives an equivariant $A \/ frak(m)^j$-linear map
  $ \( A \/ frak(m)^j \) ⊗_k T arrow.r N \/ frak(m)^j N . $
  Both modules are free of rank $dim_k T$ over the local Artinian ring $A \/ frak(m)^j$,
    and the map reduces to the identity on the residue field. Nakayama’s Lemma therefore makes it an isomorphism.
    Taking the inverse limit produces an isomorphism
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.5)")
   $ k \[ \[ x \, y \] \] ⊗_k T arrow.r^tilde.op hat(N) $ <eq:formal-trivialization>]
  compatible with the $G$-action at every finite order.
  The line $y = 0$ is fixed scheme-theoretically by $U$. Restrict (3.5) to this line and then set $K =
    k \( \( x \) \)$. This gives a rational $U_K$-isomorphism
  $ phi : T_K arrow.r^tilde.op cal(N)_(x e_1) . $
  To justify the group-scheme assertion explicitly, choose a polynomial frame for $cal(N)$ on the line;
    this is possible because a finite projective $k \[ x \]$-module is free.
    The action is a matrix $C \( x \, t \) in upright("GL")_(dim T) \( k \[ x \, t \] \)$,
    and the action on $T$ is a matrix $B \( t \) in upright("GL")_(dim T) \( k \[ t \] \)$.
    If $H \( x \)$ is the matrix of the restricted formal isomorphism, equivariance at every finite order says
  $ C \( x \, t \) H \( x \) = H \( x \) B \( t \) med \( mod med x^j \) wide upright("for every ") j gt.eq 1 . $
  These matrices have uniformly bounded degree in $t$, so coefficientwise $x$-adic separation gives the equality
    in $k \[ \[ x \] \] \[ t \]$, and hence over $K \[ t \]$.
  We must compare the two fibres without changing the parameter of $U$.
    Put $h = "diag" \( x \, x^(- 1) \) in G \( K \)$, and let $b_h : cal(N)_(e_1 \, K) arrow.r
    cal(N)_(x e_1)$ be bundle transport. Write $rho_T$ for the $G$-action on $T$ and $rho_(e_1) \,
    rho_x$ for the two fibre actions of $U$. Since $h u \( t \) h^(- 1) = u \( x^2 t \)$, equivariance gives
  $ b_h rho_(e_1) \( u \( t \) \) = rho_x \( u \( x^2 t \) \) b_h \, wide rho_T \( h \) rho_T \( u \( t \) \) =
    rho_T \( u \( x^2 t \) \) rho_T \( h \) . $
  It follows that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.6)")
   $ f = b_h^(- 1) phi rho_T \( h \) : T_K arrow.r^tilde.op cal(N)_(e_1 \, K) $ <eq:cancel-torus-conjugation>]
  intertwines the original $U$-actions. Thus restriction of $f$ to any fixed finite subgroup of $U \( k \)$ uses
    that subgroup’s original embedding.
  Finally, this isomorphism descends to $k$. In fixed $k$-bases, the intertwiner space
  $ L = "Hom"_U \( T \, cal(N)_(e_1) \) $
  is the kernel of finitely many linear equations over $k$:
    compare the coefficients of $t$ in the two polynomial action matrices.
    Hence its scalar extension to $K$ is the intertwiner space over $K$.
    Equation (3.6) shows that the determinant polynomial on $L$ is nonzero.
    Since $k$ is infinite, this polynomial is nonzero at some point of $L \( k \)$,
    giving the required isomorphism. At no point have we asserted that the formal trivialization algebraizes on
    the plane. ◻
]

#proof[
  Given $D$, choose $cal(M)$ as in Proposition 5 and put $M_0 = cal(M) \|_0$.
    Lemma 6 makes $"St"_(r - 1) ⊗ M_0$ tilting. Apply Lemma 7 to $cal(N) = "St"_(r - 1) ⊗_k cal(M)$.
    Its origin fibre is the tilting module $T = "St"_(r - 1) ⊗_k M_0$.
    By Proposition 5, the fibre of $cal(N)$ at $e_1$ restricts to $R \( "St"_(r - 1) \) ⊗
    D$ along the fixed embedding $E_r arrow.r.hook U \( k \)$. The comparison therefore gives
  $ R \( T \) tilde.equiv R \( "St"_(r - 1) \) ⊗ D \, $
  as required. This comparison also applies when $r = 1$, with $"St"_0 = k$. ◻
]

== 4 From restricted tiltings to a higher Frobenius functor <sec:higher>

Fix $r gt.eq 1$, put $q = p^r$, and choose an injective homomorphism $E = \( bb(Z) \/ p \)^r arrow.r.hook
  U \( k \)$. Write $R$ for restriction from $S L_2$, and put $cal(W) = "Ver"_(p^r) \( k \)$.
  Theorem 4 supplies the representation-theoretic input for the higher Frobenius construction of Coulembier and
  Flake. This section records that construction, explains how to evaluate it at projectives,
  and gives the exactness criterion we will use.

We will repeatedly use the following elementary fact. In a rigid abelian tensor category with simple unit,
  tensoring by a nonzero object $Y$ is exact and faithful. Indeed, the coevaluation $upright(bold(1)) arrow.r
  Y ⊗ Y^(*)$ is a nonzero monomorphism. Tensoring it by $X$ shows that $X ⊗ Y = 0$ forces $X = 0$.
  Exactness then shows that tensoring by $Y$ detects nonzero morphisms through their images.
  An exact faithful functor reflects exactness as well.

=== 4.1 The functor on group representations <the-functor-on-group-representations>

Let $cal(T)$ be the category of tilting $S L_2$-modules, and let $I_r$ be its tensor ideal generated by $"St"_r$.
  The canonical functor

$ Sigma_r : cal(T) \/ I_r arrow.r.hook cal(W) $

is fully faithful #cite(label("BEO2023")) Theorem 4.2(i).
  For every $s gt.eq 1$, $T \( a \)$ vanishes modulo $I_s$ exactly when $a gt.eq p^s -
  1$ #cite(label("BEO2023")) Section 3.1. Stable restriction factors through a faithful functor $cal(T) \/ I_r
  arrow.r "Stab" \( E \)$ #cite(label("CoulembierFlakeHigher")) Theorem 5.3.5.
  Here $"Stab" \( E \)$ denotes the quotient of $"Rep"_k \( E \)$ by morphisms factoring through projective modules.

The assertion that $R \( "St"_(r - 1) \) ⊗ D$ is a restricted tilting module for every $D in
  "Rep"_k \( E \)$ is precisely the hypothesis of #cite(label("CoulembierFlakeHigher")) Conjecture 5.4.2(1),
  Theorem 5.4.3. Since we have proved that assertion, the cited implication produces a $k$-linear strong symmetric
  monoidal functor

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.1)")
 $ Theta : "Rep"_k \( E \) arrow.r cal(W) \, wide Theta \( R \( T \) \) tilde.eq Sigma_r \( T med mod med I_r \) .
   $ <eq:theta>]

In particular, $Theta$ is additive. It need not be exact.

#thm([Lemma 4.1.])[
  #emph[If $T$ is a tilting module and $R \( T \)$ is not projective, then $Theta \( R \( T \) \) eq.not 0$.
    Moreover, $Theta$ annihilates every $E$-module induced from a proper subgroup of $E$.]
] <lem:theta-detection>

#proof[
  If $T med mod med I_r = 0$, stable restriction shows that $R \( T \)$ is projective.
    Thus nonprojectivity makes $T med mod med I_r$ nonzero; the fully faithful functor $Sigma_r$ and (4.1) give
    the first assertion.
  Stable restriction also shows that $R \( "St"_r \)$ is projective.
    Its dimension is $q$, so it is the regular module: projectives over the local algebra $k E$ are free.
    Equation (4.1) therefore shows that $Theta$ kills all projectives. Set $S = R \( "St"_(r - 1) \)$.
    Its dimension $p^(r - 1)$ is strictly smaller than $q$, so it is nonprojective and $Theta \( S \) eq.not 0$.
  Let $H < E$ have rank $s < r$. For $s > 0$, stable restriction at level $s$ shows that $"Res"_H^E
    S$ is projective, because $"St"_(r - 1) in I_s$. The assertion is automatic for $H = 1$.
    For a $k H$-module $D$, the tensor identity gives
  $ S ⊗ "Ind"_H^E D tilde.eq "Ind"_H^E \( "Res"_H^E S ⊗ D \) \, $
  which is projective. Applying $Theta$ gives $Theta \( S \) ⊗ Theta \( "Ind"_H^E D \) = 0$.
    Tensoring by a nonzero object of a tensor category is faithful, so $Theta \( "Ind"_H^E D \) = 0$. ◻
]

=== 4.2 Internal evaluation <internal-evaluation>

Let $cal(C)$ be a finite symmetric tensor category. An object of $cal(C) times.square
  "Rep"_k \( E \)$ is an object of $cal(C)$ equipped with an $E$-action. There is an internal extension of $Theta$,

$ Theta_(cal(C)) : cal(C) times.square "Rep"_k \( E \) arrow.r cal(C) times.square cal(W) . $

We explain its meaning here because $Theta$ is not assumed exact.
  Choose a projective generator $P_0$ and set $A = "End"_(cal(C)) \( P_0 \)^(upright("op"))$.
  Under $cal(C) tilde.eq A upright("-") upright("mod")$, an $E$-object is an $E$-module with a commuting
  $A$-action. Applying $Theta$ to its underlying $E$-module and to the action maps produces an $A$-module object
  in $cal(W)$, hence an object of $cal(C) times.square cal(W)$.
  This is the internal extension used in #cite(label("CoulembierFlakeHigher")) Example 3.2.4, Proposition 3.2.6,
  and Section 3.3.2; it does not require right exactness of $Theta$.

#thm([Lemma 4.2.])[
  #emph[For every projective $Q in cal(C)$ and every $E$-object $Z$ of $cal(C)$, there is a natural isomorphism
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.2)")
 $ \( "Hom"_(cal(C)) \( Q \, - \) times.square "Id"_(cal(W)) \) \( Theta_(cal(C)) \( Z \) \) tilde.eq
   Theta \( "Hom"_(cal(C)) \( Q \, Z \) \) . $ <eq:projective-evaluation>]

]
] <lem:projective-evaluation>

#proof[
  The $A$-module corresponding to $Q$ is a direct summand of $A^m$ for some $m$.
    Thus $"Hom"_A \( Q \, - \)$ is obtained from $m$ copies of the underlying object by applying a split
    idempotent. An additive functor preserves finite direct sums and split idempotents.
    Applying this observation to $Theta$ gives (4.2), including its naturality. ◻
]

=== 4.3 The tensor-power construction and exactness <the-tensor-power-construction-and-exactness>

Give $X^(⊗ q)$ the $E$-action arising from regular permutations of its tensor factors. Define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.3)")
 $ Phi \( X \) = Theta_(cal(C)) \( X^(⊗ q) \) in cal(C) times.square cal(W) . $ <eq:higher-phi>]

By Lemma 8 and #cite(label("CoulembierFlakeHigher")) Theorem 5.2.1,
  this construction has coherent strong symmetric monoidal constraints.
  They come from the $E$-equivariant reordering $\( X ⊗ Y \)^(⊗ q) tilde.eq X^(⊗ q) ⊗ Y^(⊗ q)$,
  with the diagonal action on the right, through the internal monoidal extension of
  #cite(label("CoulembierFlakeHigher")) Section 3.3.2. The resulting functor is additive and satisfies

$ Phi \( a f \) = a^q Phi \( f \) wide \( a in k \) . $

Additivity can also be seen directly from the regular action:
  in the expansion of $\( X xor Y \)^(⊗ q)$, every nonconstant coloring of the tensor positions has a proper
  stabilizer in $E$. The sum over its orbit is induced from that stabilizer, so is annihilated.
  The two constant colorings give $Phi \( X \)$ and $Phi \( Y \)$.
  The corresponding expansion on morphisms proves additivity,
  and scalar multiplication gives the displayed semilinearity.

Combining (4.3) and Lemma 9 yields

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.4)")
 $ \( "Hom"_(cal(C)) \( Q \, - \) times.square "Id"_(cal(W)) \) \( Phi \( X \) \) tilde.eq
   Theta \( "Hom"_(cal(C)) \( Q \, X^(⊗ q) \) \) . $ <eq:phi-evaluation>]

#thm([Proposition 4.3.])[
  #emph[If there are projective objects $P \, Q in cal(C)$ for which
$ Theta \( "Hom"_(cal(C)) \( Q \, P^(⊗ q) \) \) eq.not 0 \, $

 then $Phi$ is exact.]
] <prop:higher-exactness>

#proof[
  Equation (4.4) implies $Phi \( P \) eq.not 0$. Tensoring a short exact sequence by $P$ makes it split,
    since tensor products of projectives with arbitrary objects are projective.
    The additive functor $Phi$ preserves this split sequence.
    Its monoidal structure identifies the result with the image sequence tensored by $Phi \( P \)$.
    Tensoring by the nonzero object $Phi \( P \)$ is exact and faithful, and therefore reflects exactness.
    Thus the image sequence is exact. This argument is the nonzero-projective-image criterion of
    #cite(label("EtingofOstrikFrobenius")) Proposition 7.6 and Remark 7.7(i),
    applied to the higher construction as in #cite(label("CoulembierFlakeHigher")) Proposition 5.2.4. ◻
]

The next step is now concrete: we will choose $r$ and the embedding of $E$ so that

$ Theta \( "Hom"_(cal(C)) \( P \, P^(⊗ q) \) \) eq.not 0 $

for a projective generator $P$.

== 5 A first nonprojective tensor-power module <sec:projectivity>

Retain the projective generator $P$ and the exact faithful evaluation $omega =
  "Hom"_(cal(C)) \( P \, - \)$ from Lemma 2. We now select the rank of the elementary abelian group for the higher
  Frobenius construction. The essential point is that exact evaluation by a projective generator detects internal
  norm maps, even though that evaluation does not preserve tensor products.

=== 5.1 Norms and independent group actions <norms-and-independent-group-actions>

For an object $X$ with an action of a finite group $H$, denote its invariants and coinvariants by $X^H$ and $X_H$.
  These are, respectively, the simultaneous kernel and cokernel of the maps $h - 1$.
  The endomorphism $sum_(h in H) h$ factors through a morphism

$ nu_H \( X \) : X_H arrow.r X^H \, $

called the norm map. Exact $k$-linear functors preserve invariants, coinvariants, and this map.

#thm([Lemma 5.1.])[
  #emph[Let $H$ be a finite $p$-group and $M$ a finite-dimensional $k H$-module.
    Then $M$ is projective if and only if $nu_H \( M \)$ is an isomorphism.
    For an arbitrary finite group $G$, the norm map of every projective $k G$-module is an isomorphism.]
] <lem:norm-projectivity>

#proof[
  For the regular module $k G$, both coinvariants and invariants are one-dimensional,
    generated by $\[ 1 \]$ and $sum_(g in G) g$, respectively; the norm takes the first generator to the second.
    The assertion therefore holds for free modules and their direct summands.
  For the converse, $k H$ is a local self-injective algebra.
    Its unique simple module is the trivial module, and its socle is the line generated by $N_H = sum_(h in H) h$.
    The augmentation ideal is nilpotent by induction on $\| H \|$:
    choose a central element $z$ of order $p$, use $\( z - 1 \)^p = 0$,
    and pass to $k \[ H \/ chevron.l z chevron.r \]$. This proves locality.
    The nondegenerate associative pairing that takes $\( a \, b \)$ to the coefficient of $1$ in $a
    b$ identifies $k H$ with its vector-space dual as a module, proving self-injectivity.
    The socle is the space of left $H$-invariants in the regular module, hence the stated line.
    Every nonzero left ideal meets the socle. Thus, if $N_H m eq.not 0$, the map $k H arrow.r M$, $a mapsto a m$,
    is injective and splits by self-injectivity. Removing free summands repeatedly gives $M tilde.eq \( k H \)^a
    xor M'$ with $N_H M' = 0$. If $M' eq.not 0$, then $M'_H eq.not 0$ by Nakayama’s Lemma,
    so its zero norm map cannot be an isomorphism. An isomorphic norm therefore forces $M' = 0$. ◻
]

#thm([Lemma 5.2.])[
  #emph[Let finite $p$-groups $H_1 \, dots.h \, H_s$ act on objects $X_1 \, dots.h \, X_s$ of $cal(C)$,
    respectively. If each $omega \( X_i \)$ is projective over $k H_i$,
    then $omega \( X_1 ⊗ dots.h.c ⊗ X_s \)$ is projective over $k \[ H_1 times dots.h.c times H_s \]$,
    for the independent actions on the tensor factors. Its restriction to every subgroup of that product is
    projective as well.]
] <lem:internal-norm-products>

#proof[
  By Lemma 11, each evaluated norm $omega \( nu_(H_i) \( X_i \) \)$ is an isomorphism.
    Exact faithfulness of $omega$ implies that each internal norm is an isomorphism.
    Exactness of tensor products gives the natural identifications
  $ \( X ⊗ Y \)_(H times K) tilde.eq X_H ⊗ Y_K \, wide \( X ⊗ Y \)^(H times K) tilde.eq X^H ⊗ Y^K $
  when $H$ acts on $X$ and $K$ acts on $Y$. One obtains these identities by taking kernels or cokernels for one
    group at a time. Under them, $nu_(H times K) \( X ⊗ Y \) = nu_H \( X \) ⊗ nu_K \( Y \)$.
    Iteration proves that the product-group norm is an isomorphism.
    Applying $omega$ and Lemma 11 proves the first claim. Restriction preserves projectivity because a finite
    group algebra is free over the group algebra of any subgroup. ◻
]

=== 5.2 Existence and minimality <existence-and-minimality>

Let $E_a = \( bb(Z) \/ p \)^a$ act on $p^a$ positions by its regular action,
  and hence act on $P^(⊗ p^a)$ through the symmetry of $cal(C)$. Define

$ M \( a \) = omega \( P^(⊗ p^a) \) wide \( a gt.eq 1 \) . $

#thm([Proposition 5.3.])[
  #emph[There is a least integer $r gt.eq 1$ for which $M \( r \)$ is not projective over $k E_r$.
    For this $r$, the restriction of $M \( r \)$ to every proper subgroup of $E_r$ is projective.]
] <prop:first-nonprojective>

#proof[
  Suppose first that every $M \( a \)$ is projective. Fix $d gt.eq
    1$ and an elementary abelian $p$-subgroup $H subset.eq frak(S)_d$.
    On each $H$-orbit, the action is regular for a quotient $H \/ K_i$, because $H$ is abelian.
    Choose identifications $H \/ K_i tilde.eq E_(a_i)$, allowing $a_i = 0$ for singleton orbits.
    Regrouping tensor factors identifies $P^(⊗ d)$ with the tensor product of the corresponding orbit powers.
    Each evaluated orbit power is projective for its quotient group, by the assumption when $a_i > 0$,
    and trivially when $a_i = 0$. Lemma 12 applies to the independent product of the quotient groups.
    The homomorphism $H arrow.r product_i H \/ K_i$ is injective, since the permutation action of $H$ is faithful.
    Restriction along it shows that $omega \( P^(⊗ d) \)$ is projective over $k H$.
    Chouinard’s projectivity theorem now implies that $omega \( P^(⊗ d) \)$ is projective over $k
    frak(S)_d$ #cite(label("Chouinard1976")); see also #cite(label("Benson2015")) p. 20.
  The tensor power of $P arrow.r.twohead upright(bold(1))$ is an $frak(S)_d$-equivariant epimorphism to the unit
    with its trivial action. Exact evaluation, followed by coinvariants, therefore gives an epimorphism
  $ omega \( P^(⊗ d) \)_(frak(S)_d) arrow.r.twohead omega \( upright(bold(1)) \) eq.not 0 . $
  Lemma 11 shows that the total symmetrizer $b_d = sum_(sigma in frak(S)_d)
    sigma$ acts nontrivially on $omega \( P^(⊗ d) \)$, and hence on $P^(⊗ d)$.
  On the other hand, the action maps
  $ k frak(S)_d arrow.r "End"_(cal(C)) \( P^(⊗ d) \) $
  cannot all be injective. To see this without any dimension theory,
    write $ell \( X \)$ for the length of $X$ and put $b = max_i ell \( S_i ⊗ P \)$.
    Exactness gives $ell \( P^(⊗ d) \) lt.eq ell \( P \) b^(d - 1)$.
    Since the simples have endomorphism field $k$, induction on two composition series gives $dim_k
    "Hom" \( X \, Y \) lt.eq ell \( X \) ell \( Y \)$. Thus $dim_k
    "End" \( P^(⊗ d) \)$ grows at most exponentially, whereas $dim_k k frak(S)_d = d !$.
  For clarity, the kernels form a tensor ideal in the permutation category $sans(S y m)$:
    its objects are the nonnegative integers, its endomorphism algebra at $d$ is $k frak(S)_d$,
    other morphism spaces are zero, and tensor product is addition with the usual block inclusions of symmetric
    groups. The symmetry of $cal(C)$ gives a monoidal functor $sans(S y m) arrow.r cal(C)$, $d mapsto P^(⊗ d)$,
    and the kernels form its nonzero kernel ideal. By #cite(label("CoulembierEtingofOstrik2023")) Lemma 4.10,
    every nonzero tensor ideal in $sans(S y m)$ contains some total symmetrizer $b_d$ in positive characteristic.
    Its proof uses Kleshchev’s faithfulness result in the appendix to that paper
    #cite(label("CoulembierEtingofOstrik2023")) Appendix A, Proposition A.1.
    This contradicts the preceding nonvanishing. The least $r$ therefore exists.
  Finally, let $H < E_r$ be proper, of rank $a < r$. Its action on the regular $E_r$-set is a disjoint union of
    regular $H$-orbits. Each orbit power has projective evaluation by minimality when $a > 0$,
    and trivially when $a = 0$. Apply Lemma 12 to the independent orbit groups,
    then restrict to their diagonal copy of $H$. This proves the last assertion, including $r = 1$. ◻
]

The proposition supplies a nonprojective $E_r$-module whose restriction to every proper subgroup is projective.
  The next step uses its support to choose the embedding $E_r arrow.r.hook bb(G)_a$.

== 6 Selecting an embedding by rank support <sec:support>

The preceding section supplies a nonprojective representation of an elementary abelian group whose restrictions to
  all proper subgroups are projective. We now choose an embedding of that group in the unipotent subgroup of
  $upright("SL")_2$ so that its tensor product with the restricted Steinberg module remains nonprojective.
  This choice will make the higher Frobenius functor exact.

Let $k$ be algebraically closed of characteristic $p > 0$, let $E_r = \( bb(Z) \/ p bb(Z) \)^r$,
  and fix generators $g_1 \, dots.h \, g_r$. For a finite-dimensional $k E_r$-module $D$, define

$ "Supp" \( D \) = {\[ alpha_1 : dots.h.c : alpha_r \] in bb(P)^(r - 1) \( k \) :
  D upright(" is not free over ") k \[ t \] \/ \( t^p \) \, quad t mapsto sum_(i = 1)^r alpha_i \( g_i - 1 \)} . $

This is the projectivized rank variety. The rank-variety projectivity and tensor-product theorems state that

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.1)")
 $ "Supp" \( D \) = diameter arrow.l.r.double D upright(" is projective") \, wide "Supp" \( D ⊗ D' \) =
   "Supp" \( D \) ∩ "Supp" \( D' \) . $ <eq:support-properties>]

These statements hold with the indicated coordinates for every prime;
  see #cite(label("Benson2017")) Sections 1.9 and 1.12 and #cite(label("CoulembierFlakeSteinberg")) Section 2.3.

#thm([Lemma 6.1.])[
  #emph[Suppose that $M$ is a nonprojective finite-dimensional $k
    E_r$-module and that $M \|_H$ is projective for every proper subgroup $H < E_r$.
    Every point $\[ alpha_1 : dots.h.c : alpha_r \]$ of $"Supp" \( M \)$ has $bb(F)_p$-linearly independent
    coordinates.]
] <lem:support-independent>

#proof[
  For a nonzero linear form $c : E_r arrow.r bb(F)_p$, write $c \( g_i \) = c_i$ and put $H = ker c$.
    The tensor identity gives
  $ M ⊗ "Ind"_H^(E_r) k tilde.equiv "Ind"_H^(E_r) \( M \|_H \) \, $
  which is projective. We compute the support of $N = "Ind"_H^(E_r) k$ explicitly.
    This is the regular representation of $E_r \/ H$, identified with $k \[ z \] \/ \( z^p \)$,
    where a generator of the quotient acts by $1 + z$. Thus $sum_i alpha_i \( g_i - 1 \)$ acts by multiplication by
  $ h_alpha \( z \) = sum_i alpha_i #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( 1 + z \)^(c_i) - 1 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, $
  with $c_i$ represented by an integer in ${ 0 \, dots.h \, p - 1 }$.
    Its linear coefficient is $sum_i c_i alpha_i$. If this coefficient is nonzero,
    substitution $t mapsto h_alpha \( z \)$ is an isomorphism of truncated polynomial algebras,
    so $N$ is free over $k \[ t \] \/ \( t^p \)$. If it is zero,
    then $h_alpha in \( z^2 \)$ and $h_alpha^(p - 1) = 0$, which is impossible on a free module of dimension $p$.
    Consequently
  $ "Supp" \( N \) = { \[ alpha \] : sum_i c_i alpha_i = 0 } . $
  Equation (6.1) shows that this hyperplane misses $"Supp" \( M \)$.
    Varying the nonzero vector $\( c_1 \, dots.h \, c_r \) in bb(F)_p^r$ proves the assertion. ◻
]

Every support point of the minimal-rank module therefore has $bb(F)_p$-linearly independent coordinates.
  To make one of them survive after tensoring with a restricted Steinberg module,
  we next compute the Steinberg support in terms of the embedding parameters.

#thm([Lemma 6.2.])[
  #emph[Let $lambda_1 \, dots.h \, lambda_r in k$ be linearly independent over $bb(F)_p$,
    and restrict $upright("SL")_2$-representations along
$ iota_lambda : E_r arrow.r upright("SL")_2 \( k \) \, wide g_i mapsto mat(delim: "(", 1, lambda_i; 0, 1) . $

 Write $R_lambda$ for this restriction. Then
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.2)")
 $ "Supp" #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] R_lambda \( "St"_(r - 1) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = {\[ alpha \] :
   sum_i alpha_i lambda_i^(p^j) = 0 quad \( 0 lt.eq j lt.eq r - 2 \)} . $ <eq:steinberg-support>]

]
] <lem:steinberg-support>

#proof[
  This is the Steinberg case of #cite(label("CoulembierFlakeSteinberg")) Proposition 4.2.5 and Corollary 4.2.7.
    Here is a direct calculation in the chosen coordinates. On $"St"_1 = "Sym"^(p - 1) \( k^2 \)$,
    the upper unipotent element with parameter $a$ acts by $exp \( a N \) = sum_(b = 0)^(p - 1) a^b N^b \/ b !$,
    where $N$ has one Jordan block of length $p$. Therefore $sum_i
    alpha_i \( g_i - 1 \)$ acts by a polynomial in $N$ whose linear coefficient is $sum_i alpha_i lambda_i$.
    As in the proof of Lemma 14, the resulting module of dimension $p$ is free exactly when this coefficient is
    nonzero. On the $j$-th Frobenius twist, the same calculation replaces $lambda_i$ by $lambda_i^(p^j)$. Now use
  $ "St"_(r - 1) tilde.equiv ⨂_(j = 0)^(r - 2) "St"_1^(\( j \)) $
  and the tensor-product formula in (6.1). When $r = 1$, $"St"_0 = k$ has support $bb(P)^0 \( k \)$,
    agreeing with the empty system in (6.2). ◻
]

Thus a point $\[ alpha \]$ lies in the Steinberg support precisely when the embedding parameters satisfy the
  equations in (6.2). The next lemma solves these equations while keeping the parameters linearly independent over
  $bb(F)_p$, as required for an embedding.

#thm([Lemma 6.3.])[
  #emph[Let $alpha_1 \, dots.h \, alpha_r in k$ be linearly independent over $bb(F)_p$.
    There are $bb(F)_p$-linearly independent $lambda_1 \, dots.h \, lambda_r in k$ such that
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.3)")
 $ sum_(i = 1)^r alpha_i lambda_i^(p^j) = 0 wide \( 0 lt.eq j lt.eq r - 2 \) . $ <eq:selected-embedding>]

 For $r = 1$ the list of equations is empty.]
] <lem:moore-selection>

#proof[
  When $r = 1$, take any $lambda_1 eq.not 0$. Suppose $r gt.eq 2$.
    Because $k$ is perfect, we may put $beta_i = alpha_i^(p^(- \( r - 2 \)))$. Form the $\( r - 1 \) times r$ matrix
  $ B = \( beta_i^(p^s) \)_(0 lt.eq s lt.eq r - 2\
1 lt.eq i lt.eq r) . $
  Its rows are linearly independent. Indeed, a nontrivial row relation would give a nonzero additive polynomial
    $f \( X \) = sum_(s = 0)^(r - 2) a_s X^(p^s)$ vanishing on the $bb(F)_p$-span of the $beta_i$.
    That span has $p^r$ elements, whereas $deg f lt.eq p^(r - 2)$.
  The row space of $B$ contains no nonzero vector in $bb(F)_p^r$.
    To see this, suppose $c_i = f \( beta_i \) in bb(F)_p$ for such a polynomial $f$.
    The additive polynomial $f^p - f$ then vanishes on the same $p^r$ elements and has degree at most $p^(r - 1)$.
    Thus $f^p - f = 0$, forcing $f = 0$ because $f$ has zero constant term.
  Choose a nonzero vector $lambda in ker B$. This kernel is a line.
    If $c in bb(F)_p^r$ satisfied $sum_i c_i lambda_i = 0$, it would belong to the annihilator of $ker B$,
    which is the row space of $B$. Hence $c = 0$, proving the required independence of the $lambda_i$.
    The rows of $B$, in reverse order, are $\( alpha_i^(p^(- j)) \)_i$ for $0 lt.eq j lt.eq r - 2$.
    Raising their nullvector equations to the $p^j$-th power gives (6.3). ◻
]

#thm([Proposition 6.4.])[
  #emph[Let $M$ be a nonprojective finite-dimensional $k E_r$-module whose restriction to every proper subgroup is
    projective. There is an embedding $iota_lambda : E_r arrow.r.hook
    upright("SL")_2 \( k \)$ into the upper unipotent subgroup such that
$ R_lambda \( "St"_(r - 1) \) ⊗ M quad upright("is nonprojective") . $

]
] <prop:support-selection>

#proof[
  By (6.1), choose $\[ alpha \] in "Supp" \( M \)$. Lemma 14 makes its coordinates $bb(F)_p$-linearly independent.
    Choose $lambda$ by Lemma 16. Its independent coordinates make $iota_lambda$ injective,
    and Lemma 15 places $\[ alpha \]$ in the support of $R_lambda \( "St"_(r - 1) \)$.
    The tensor-product theorem now gives the conclusion. ◻
]

#thm([Theorem 6.5.])[
  (An exact higher Frobenius functor).  #emph[Let $cal(C)$ be a finite symmetric tensor category over an
    algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $p > 0$.
    There are an integer $r gt.eq 1$ and an embedding $E_r arrow.r.hook
    U \( k \)$ for which the construction of Section 4 gives an exact additive strong symmetric monoidal functor
$ Phi : cal(C) arrow.r cal(C) times.square "Ver"_(p^r) \( k \) . $

 It is given by $Phi \( X \) = Theta_(cal(C)) \( X^(⊗ p^r) \)$,
   where $Theta$ is the functor in (4.1) for the chosen embedding.
   The functor $Phi$ satisfies $Phi \( a f \) = a^(p^r) Phi \( f \)$,
   and its evaluation at every projective $Q in cal(C)$ is given by the natural isomorphism (4.4).]
] <thm:exact-higher-frobenius>

#proof[
  Choose a projective generator $P$ and the least rank $r$ of Proposition 13. Then
  $ M = "Hom"_(cal(C)) \( P \, P^(⊗ p^r) \) $
  is nonprojective and has projective restriction to every proper subgroup of $E_r$.
    Proposition 17 supplies an embedding $iota_lambda$ for which $R_lambda \( "St"_(r - 1) \) ⊗
    M$ remains nonprojective. Theorem 4 gives $R_lambda \( "St"_(r - 1) \) ⊗ M tilde.equiv
    R_lambda \( T \)$ for a tilting module $T$. Lemma 8 therefore gives
  $ 0 eq.not Theta #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] R_lambda \( T \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] tilde.equiv Theta
    #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] R_lambda \( "St"_(r - 1) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] ⊗ Theta \( M \) . $
  In particular $Theta \( M \) eq.not 0$. Proposition 10, with both projectives equal to $P$,
    proves that $Phi$ is exact. Its remaining properties and the projective-evaluation identity were established
    in Section 4. All steps include $p = 2$ and $r = 1$. ◻
]

== 7 Removing the original category from the target <sec:dimension-descent>

Choose an exact higher Frobenius functor by Theorem 18. Write $q = p^r$, $cal(W) = "Ver"_q \( k \)$, and retain

$ Phi : cal(C) arrow.r cal(C) times.square cal(W) . $

The functor is $q$-power semilinear, exact, and strong symmetric monoidal.
  Its evaluation property says that, for every projective $Q in cal(C)$,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.1)")
 $ \( "Hom"_(cal(C)) \( Q \, - \) times.square "Id" \) \( Phi \( X \) \) tilde.eq Theta
   #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] "Hom"_(cal(C)) \( Q \, X^(⊗ q) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] .
   $ <eq:descent-evaluation>]

Here $Theta : "Rep"_k \( E_r \) arrow.r cal(W)$ is the $k$-linear strong symmetric monoidal functor used in its
  construction. Our objective is to show that sufficiently many iterations of $Phi$ place the first factor in the
  Serre closure of a Frobenius-exact subcategory. The Serre closure is the smallest full subcategory closed under
  subobjects, quotients, and extensions that contains it.

=== 7.1 Ordinary Frobenius and a Grothendieck-ring identity <ordinary-frobenius-and-a-grothendieck-ring-identity>

For an object $Y in cal(C)$, let $c$ denote the cyclic permutation of the $p$ factors of $Y^(⊗ p)$, and set $D =
  1 - c$. Thus $D^p = 0$. The components of the ordinary Frobenius functor are

$ F_i \( Y \) = frac(ker D ∩ "im" D^(i - 1), ker D ∩ "im" D^i) \, wide 1 lt.eq i < p . $

If $ell_i$ is the simple object of $"Ver"_p \( k \)$ represented by a Jordan block of length $i$, the functor is

$ F \( Y \) = xor.big_(i = 1)^(p - 1) F_i \( Y \) times.square ell_i . $

We use the semilinear convention, so the objects $F_i \( Y \)$ belong to $cal(C)$.
  This construction and its monoidal structure are due to Etingof–Ostrik #cite(label("EtingofOstrikFrobenius"))
  Sections 3–5. It need not be exact.

Let $K_0 \( cal(C) \)$ be the Grothendieck ring, with a basis given by simple classes,
  addition induced by short exact sequences, and multiplication induced by tensor product.
  Symmetry makes it commutative. Frobenius–Perron dimension $d$ is its unique positive normalized real character:
  it is additive on exact sequences, multiplicative on tensor products, and equals one on the unit.
  Each simple object has dimension at least one. On a Deligne product it is the product of the two dimensions;
  see #cite(label("EGNO2015")) Sections 3.3 and 4.5. We need two results from
  #cite(label("EtingofOstrikFrobenius")) Propositions 6.3 and 7.4:

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.2)")
 $ d \( F \( Y \) \) lt.eq d \( Y \) \, wide cal(C)_(upright("ex")) = { Y in cal(C) : d \( F \( Y \) \) =
   d \( Y \) } . $ <eq:ordinary-dimension>]

The ordinary Frobenius functor is exact on $cal(C)_(upright("ex"))$, which is a tensor subcategory.
  Such a category is called #emph[Frobenius exact]. These statements require only finitely many simple isomorphism
  classes. The tensor subcategory is closed under subquotients and duals; extension closure is not asserted here.

The next identity will connect the two Frobenius constructions:
  a composition factor of $Y^(⊗ p^r)$ whose multiplicity is nonzero modulo $p$ must occur in an iterated ordinary
  Frobenius component.

#thm([Lemma 7.1.])[
  #emph[In the commutative ring $K_0 \( cal(C) \) \/ p K_0 \( cal(C) \)$, one has
$ \[ Y \]^p = sum_(i = 1)^(p - 1) i \[ F_i \( Y \) \] . $

 Consequently, for every $r gt.eq 1$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.3)")
 $ \[ Y \]^(p^r) =
   sum_(i_1 \, dots.h \, i_r = 1)^(p - 1) \( i_1 dots.h.c i_r \) \[ F_(i_r) dots.h.c F_(i_1) \( Y \) \]
   med \( mod med p \) . $ <eq:iterated-ordinary-congruence>]

]
] <lem:ordinary-congruence>

#proof[
  Put $Z = Y^(⊗ p)$ and $K_j = ker D ∩ "im" D^j$, for $0 lt.eq j lt.eq p$. The exact sequences
  $ 0 arrow.r K_j arrow.r "im" D^j arrow.r^D "im" D^(j + 1) arrow.r 0 wide \( 0 lt.eq j < p \) $
  give $\[ Z \] = sum_(j = 0)^(p - 1) \[ K_j \]$. Since $K_0 supset.eq dots.h.c supset.eq K_p = 0$,
    summing the successive quotients in these filtrations gives the integral identity
  $ \[ Z \] = sum_(i = 1)^(p - 1) i \[ F_i \( Y \) \] + p \[ K_(p - 1) \] . $
  Reduction modulo $p$ proves the first assertion. For the second,
    raise this identity to successive $p$-th powers in the commutative ring $K_0 \( cal(C) \) \/ p$,
    and apply the first assertion to each concrete object $F_(i_s) dots.h.c F_(i_1) \( Y \)$.
    This argument does not apply a nonexact functor to a Grothendieck-group relation. ◻
]

=== 7.2 Equality in dimension forces Frobenius exactness <equality-in-dimension-forces-frobenius-exactness>

An exact strong monoidal functor between finite tensor categories preserves Frobenius–Perron dimension,
  also when its semilinearity is a field automorphism. Indeed, its map on Grothendieck rings preserves the unit,
  products, and positive classes. It sends nonzero objects to nonzero objects:
  apply exactness to the monomorphism $upright(bold(1)) arrow.r X ⊗ X^(*)$ for $X eq.not 0$.
  Composing with dimension therefore gives a positive normalized character;
  the Perron–Frobenius characterization makes it the dimension character of the source #cite(label("EGNO2015"))
  Propositions 3.3.6, 3.3.13, and 4.5.7. In particular,

$ d \( Phi \( X \) \) = d \( X \) . $

The simple objects of $cal(C) times.square cal(W)$ are precisely $A times.square B$, with $A \, B$ simple,
  and their dimensions are $d \( A \) d \( B \)$.

#thm([Lemma 7.2.])[
  #emph[Let $S$ be a simple object of $cal(C)$, and let $A times.square
    B$ be a composition factor of $Phi \( S \)$. Then $d \( A \) lt.eq d \( S \)$. If equality holds, then
$ Phi \( S \) tilde.eq A times.square B \, wide B upright(" is invertible") \, wide S in cal(C)_(upright("ex")) . $

 In this case $A$ is the unique first component of a composition factor of $Phi \( S \)$.]
] <lem:equality-descent>

#proof[
  Every nonzero object has dimension at least one. Thus $d \( S \) = d \( Phi \( S \) \) gt.eq
    d \( A \) d \( B \) gt.eq d \( A \)$. If the two ends are equal,
    positivity of the dimensions of all composition factors shows that $Phi \( S \)$ has length one and
    $d \( B \) = 1$. A simple object of dimension one is invertible,
    since $B ⊗ B^(*)$ has dimension one and contains the unit.
  Let $Q_A$ be the projective cover of $A$. Over the algebraically closed field $k$,
  $ dim_k "Hom"_(cal(C)) \( Q_A \, X \) = \[ X : A \] $
  for every $X$: the functor is exact and has dimension one on $A$ and zero on every other simple object.
    Applying (7.1) to $S$ gives
  $ Theta #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] "Hom"_(cal(C)) \( Q_A \, S^(⊗ q) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] tilde.eq B . $
  A symmetric monoidal functor preserves categorical dimension.
    This dimension of the representation on the left is its vector-space dimension viewed in $k$,
    whereas the categorical dimension of the invertible object $B$ is nonzero:
    its product with the dimension of $B^(*)$ is one. Consequently,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.4)")
   $ \[ S^(⊗ q) : A \] equiv.not 0 med \( mod med p \) . $ <eq:multiplicity-nonzero>]
  This uses categorical dimension only to establish the congruence;
    the real-valued dimension $d$ remains Frobenius–Perron dimension.
  By (7.3) and (7.4), there are indices $i_1 \, dots.h \, i_r in { 1 \, dots.h \, p -
    1 }$ such that $A$ is a composition factor of $Y_r$, where $Y_0 = S$ and $Y_t = F_(i_t) \( Y_(t - 1) \)$.
    For every $t$, Equation (7.2) and $d \( ell_(i_t) \) gt.eq 1$ give
  $ d \( Y_(t - 1) \) gt.eq d \( F \( Y_(t - 1) \) \) gt.eq d \( Y_t \) . $
  Since $d \( Y_r \) gt.eq d \( A \) = d \( S \)$, all inequalities are equalities.
    In particular $d \( F \( S \) \) = d \( S \)$, so $S in cal(C)_(upright("ex"))$. ◻
]

===== 7.2.1 The finite graph. <the-finite-graph.>

We adapt the finite-simple dimension descent used by Etingof–Ostrik for ordinary Frobenius iteration
  #cite(label("EtingofOstrikFrobenius")) Proposition 10.1 to the exact functor $Phi$.
  Let $N$ be the number of simple isomorphism classes in $cal(C)$.
  Form a directed graph on these classes, putting an arrow $S arrow.r
  A$ when $A times.square B$ occurs in $Phi \( S \)$ for some simple $B$. Every vertex has an outgoing arrow.
  By Lemma 20, dimension is nonincreasing along arrows. On a directed cycle it is constant,
  every vertex belongs to $cal(C)_(upright("ex"))$, and every vertex has just one outgoing neighbor.
  Thus a path entering a cycle cannot leave it. A path of $N$ edges visits $N + 1$ vertices,
  so it contains such a cycle. Its endpoint therefore belongs to $cal(C)_(upright("ex"))$.

Figure 1 illustrates this trapping mechanism: the unique outgoing neighbor at each cycle vertex prevents a path
  from leaving the cycle, even though dimension need not decrease strictly before the cycle.

