=== 7.3 Scalar twists and iteration <subsec:scalar-iteration>

Put $sigma \( a \) = a^q$ for $a in k$, where $q = p^r$ is the power chosen above.
  Since $k$ is algebraically closed, $sigma$ is an automorphism.
  A functor is $sigma$-semilinear if $F \( a f \) = sigma \( a \)
  F \( f \)$ for every scalar $a$ and morphism $f$. We now define the iteration of $Phi$ without mixing different
  scalar actions.

For a $k$-linear category $cal(A)$, let $cal(A)^(\[ sigma \])$ have the same objects and additive morphism groups,
  with scalar action

$ a dot.op_(\[ sigma \]) f = sigma^(- 1) \( a \) f . $

Thus a $sigma$-semilinear functor $cal(A) arrow.r cal(B)$ is precisely a $k$-linear functor $cal(A)^(\[ sigma \])
  arrow.r cal(B)$. For finite abelian categories the universal property of the Deligne product gives

$ \( cal(A) times.square cal(B) \)^(\[ sigma \]) tilde.eq cal(A)^(\[ sigma \]) times.square cal(B)^(\[ sigma \]) . $

Consequently two functors with the same semilinearity have a well-defined Deligne product with that semilinearity:
  apply the ordinary Deligne product to their $k$-linear versions after this identification.

Every higher Verlinde category is defined over the prime field #cite(label("BEO2023")) Section 4.9;
  the resulting scalar equivalences are also recorded in #cite(label("CoulembierFlakeHigher")) Remark 2.3.8.
  Hence, for every integer $a$, scalar transport along $b mapsto
  b^(p^a)$ gives an exact strong symmetric semilinear autoequivalence of $"Ver"_(p^s) \( k \)$.
  Here negative powers denote inverse Frobenius automorphisms of $k$.
  Indeed, scalar transport acts on a presentation of the category and its tensor structure over $bb(F)_p$;
  all structure maps are preserved because the automorphism fixes $bb(F)_p$.
  Its inverse is transport along the inverse field automorphism.
  These autoequivalences are scalar transports, distinct from the ordinary Frobenius functor, which need not be exact.

Let $J_sigma$ denote such an autoequivalence of $cal(W) = "Ver"_q \( k \)$ with semilinearity $sigma$.
  Set $Psi_0 = "Id"_(cal(C))$ and $Psi_1 = Phi$. For $j gt.eq 1$, define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.5)")
 $ Psi_(j + 1) = #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] Phi times.square J_sigma^(times.square j)
   #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] compose Psi_j : cal(C) arrow.r
   cal(C) times.square cal(W)^(times.square \( j + 1 \)) . $ <eq:scalar-iteration>]

Each factor in the parenthesized functor has semilinearity $sigma$, so the product is defined as above.
  By induction $Psi_j$ is exact, strong symmetric monoidal, and $sigma^j$-semilinear.
  The old $cal(W)$-factors are only transported by equivalences.
  We now apply the graph argument to the first components of its composition factors.

=== 7.4 The image after iteration <the-image-after-iteration>

The scalar-compatible iteration (7.5) gives an exact functor

$ Psi_N : cal(C) arrow.r cal(C) times.square cal(W)^(times.square N) . $

Exactness is essential here: applying the next functor to a composition series shows that every first component of
  every composition factor follows a path of one additional edge.
  The autoequivalences on the other factors send simple objects to simple objects and do not change the first
  component. Induction, first for simple inputs and then for a composition series of an arbitrary input,
  shows that all first components of $Psi_N \( X \)$ belong to $cal(C)_(upright("ex"))$.

Let $cal(D)$ be the full subcategory of $cal(C)$ consisting of objects whose simple composition factors belong to
  $cal(C)_(upright("ex"))$. This is the Serre closure of $cal(C)_(upright("ex"))$.
  It is closed under tensor products, because biexactness gives tensor-product filtrations and
  $cal(C)_(upright("ex"))$ is a tensor subcategory closed under subquotients. Exact duality also preserves it.
  Hence $cal(D)$ inherits the rigid symmetric tensor structure and the simple unit.

For completeness, the relevant abelian and Deligne-product assertions have a concrete finite-algebra description.
  Choose a basic finite dimensional algebra $H$ with $cal(C) tilde.eq H upright("-") upright("mod")$,
  and let $e$ be the sum of the primitive idempotents corresponding to the simple objects outside
  $cal(C)_(upright("ex"))$. The functor $M mapsto e M$ is exact, and vanishes on exactly the allowed simples. Thus

$ cal(D) tilde.eq \( H \/ H e H \) upright("-") upright("mod") . $

In particular $cal(D)$ is finite and has enough projectives.
  If $cal(W)^(times.square N) tilde.eq J upright("-") upright("mod")$, then

$ cal(D) times.square cal(W)^(times.square N) tilde.eq #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( H \/ H e H \) ⊗_k J
  #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] upright("-") upright("mod") $

is the full Serre subcategory of $\( H ⊗_k J \) upright("-") upright("mod")$ annihilated by $e ⊗ 1$.
  It consists exactly of objects with allowed first components.
  We have therefore obtained an exact strong symmetric monoidal functor

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.6)")
 $ Psi_N : cal(C) arrow.r cal(D) times.square cal(W)^(times.square N) . $ <eq:descent-result>]

The remaining step is to fiber $cal(D)$ over a small Verlinde category and to remove the total scalar twist.

=== 7.5 A fiber functor on the Serre closure <subsec:serre-fiber>

It remains to replace $cal(D)$ in Equation (7.6) by a fixed low Verlinde level and correct the scalar action.

We first check finiteness for the subcategories in the fiber-functor theorem.
  More generally, let $cal(B)$ be a full additive subcategory of $A upright("-")
  upright("mod")$ closed under subobjects and quotients, where $A$ is finite dimensional.
  Put $I = ⋂_(M in cal(B)) "Ann"_A \( M \)$. Since $A$ is finite dimensional,
  this intersection is already a finite intersection. A finite direct sum $M in
  cal(B)$ therefore satisfies $"Ann"_A \( M \) = I$. Give $"End"_k \( M \)$ the left $A$-action by
  postcomposition: $a dot.op u = rho_M \( a \) compose u$. The action map embeds the left regular $A \/
  I$-module in $"End"_k \( M \) tilde.equiv M^(xor dim_k M)$, so $A \/ I in cal(B)$.
  Every finite $A \/ I$-module is a quotient of a finite direct sum of $A \/ I$.
  Thus $cal(B) = \( A \/ I \) upright("-") upright("mod")$ and, in particular,
  $cal(B)$ is finite abelian and has enough projectives. This applies to $cal(C)_(upright("ex"))$,
  which is a tensor subcategory closed under subquotients #cite(label("EtingofOstrikFrobenius")) Proposition 7.4.
  The inherited tensor product and duality therefore make $cal(C)_(upright("ex"))$ a finite symmetric tensor
  category; these properties for $cal(D)$ were established above.

By #cite(label("EtingofOstrikFrobenius")) Theorem 8.1, the Frobenius-exact category $cal(C)_(upright("ex"))$ has a
  $k$-linear fiber functor to $"Ver"_p \( k \)$. If $p > 2$,
  its Serre closure equals $cal(C)_(upright("ex"))$ #cite(label("EtingofOstrikFrobenius")) Corollary 9.5;
  hence the same is true of $cal(D)$.

If $p = 2$, the preceding fiber functor has target $"Ver"_2 \( k \) = "Vec"_k$.
  The finite tensor subcategory $cal(C)_(upright("ex")) subset.eq
  cal(D)$ contains every simple object of $cal(D)$, by the definition of $cal(D)$.
  Thus the extension theorem of Etingof–Gelaki #cite(label("EtingofGelaki2021")) Theorem 2.21(2) applies to this
  inclusion and gives a $k$-linear fiber functor $cal(D) arrow.r cal(V)$,
  exactly as in #cite(label("EtingofOstrikFrobenius")) Corollary 9.8. Here

$ cal(V) = "Rep"_k #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] k \[ d \] \/ \( d^2 \) \, thin Delta \( d \) = d ⊗ 1 + 1 ⊗ d \,
  thin R = 1 ⊗ 1 + d ⊗ d #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $

In that result, “almost Frobenius exact” means precisely the existence of this exact faithful symmetric tensor
  functor #cite(label("EtingofOstrikFrobenius")) Definition 8.13.
  The category $cal(V)$ is the category $cal(C)_1$ of #cite(label("BensonEtingof2019")) Introduction;
  by #cite(label("BEO2023")) Theorem 4.5(iii),

$ cal(V) tilde.eq "Ver"_4^(+) \( k \) subset "Ver"_4 \( k \) $

as symmetric tensor categories. This is the only additional level needed in characteristic two.

=== 7.6 Completion of the fiber functor <subsec:completion>

Set $n = max \( 2 \, r \)$ and $cal(Z) = "Ver"_(p^n) \( k \)$.
  The exact symmetric tower inclusions #cite(label("BEO2023")) Theorem 4.34,
  together with the preceding fiber functor, give $k$-linear exact strong symmetric functors

$ E : cal(D) arrow.r cal(Z) \, wide I : cal(W) arrow.r cal(Z) . $

They combine to a $k$-linear exact strong symmetric functor

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.7)")
 $ H : cal(D) times.square cal(W)^(times.square N) arrow.r cal(Z) \, wide
   H \( D times.square W_1 times.square dots.h.c times.square W_N \) =
   E \( D \) ⊗ I \( W_1 \) ⊗ dots.h.c ⊗ I \( W_N \) . $ <eq:combined-functor>]

Exactness and the symmetric tensor structure follow from Lemma 3.
  The composite $H Psi_N$ has semilinearity $sigma^N$. Let $J_(sigma^(- N))$ be scalar transport on $cal(Z)$ with
  semilinearity $sigma^(- N)$. Then

$ cal(F) = J_(sigma^(- N)) compose H compose Psi_N : cal(C) arrow.r "Ver"_(p^n) \( k \) $

is $k$-linear, exact, and strong symmetric monoidal. Finally,
  an exact strong monoidal functor between tensor categories with simple units is faithful.
  Indeed, if $X eq.not 0$, the coevaluation $upright(bold(1)) arrow.r
  X ⊗ X^(*)$ is nonzero by a triangle identity, hence injective by simplicity of $upright(bold(1))$.
  Its exact image shows that $cal(F) \( X \) eq.not 0$. For a nonzero morphism $f$,
  exactness gives $"im" cal(F) \( f \) tilde.equiv cal(F) \( "im" f \) eq.not 0$.
  Thus $cal(F)$ is the required fiber functor, proving Theorem 1.

== 8 Classification, uniqueness, and reconstruction <sec:structural-consequences>

We combine Theorem 1 with the established structure of the higher Verlinde categories and generalized Tannaka
  duality. The resulting statements concern finite symmetric tensor categories;
  the uniqueness assertions apply in characteristic two.

=== 8.1 Finite incompressible symmetric tensor categories <finite-incompressible-symmetric-tensor-categories>

For a finite symmetric tensor category $cal(C)$ over $k$,
  #emph[incompressibility] means that every $k$-linear exact strong symmetric monoidal functor from $cal(C)$ to a
  symmetric tensor category over $k$ is fully faithful. This is the finite-source form of incompressibility in
  #cite(label("CoulembierEtingofOstrikIncompressible")) Section 3.2.
  The symmetry condition is part of the definition here.

Write $"Vec"_k$ for finite-dimensional vector spaces and, when $p > 2$,
  $"sVec"_k$ for finite-dimensional supervector spaces with the sign symmetry.
  For $p^m > 2$, $"Ver"_(p^m)^(+) \( k \)$ denotes the neutral component of the $bb(Z) \/
  2$-grading constructed from the even highest-weight tilting modules in #cite(label("BEO2023")) Section 4.1.
  The low levels satisfy

$ "Ver"_2 \( k \) tilde.eq "Vec"_k \, wide "Ver"_3 \( k \) tilde.eq "sVec"_k \, wide
  "Ver"_3^(+) \( k \) tilde.eq "Vec"_k \; $

see #cite(label("BEO2023")) Theorem 4.5(iii), Corollary 4.11, and Example 4.52.
  In particular, the plus notation is used only when $p^m > 2$.

#thm([Corollary 8.1.])[
  (Finite incompressible classification).  #emph[Let $k$ be algebraically closed of characteristic $p > 0$.
    A finite symmetric tensor category over $k$ is incompressible if and only if it is equivalent as a $k$-linear
    symmetric tensor category to a category in the corresponding row:
$ p = 2 & "Ver"_(2^m) \( k \) med \( m gt.eq 1 \) \, quad "Ver"_(2^m)^(+) \( k \) med \( m gt.eq 2 \) \,\
p = 3 & "Ver"_(3^m) \( k \) \, med "Ver"_(3^m)^(+) \( k \) quad \( m gt.eq 1 \) \,\
p gt.eq 5 & "Vec"_k \, med "sVec"_k \, quad "Ver"_(p^m) \( k \) \, med "Ver"_(p^m)^(+) \( k \) quad \( m gt.eq 1 \) . $

]
] <cor:incompressible-classification>

#proof[
  Let $cal(C)$ be incompressible and choose a fiber functor $F : cal(C) arrow.r "Ver"_(p^n) \( k \)$ by Theorem 1.
    Let $cal(I)$ be the full subcategory of subquotients of objects $F \( X \)$, $X in cal(C)$.
    Because $cal(C)$ is finite, $cal(I)$ is a finite tensor subcategory and the corestriction $F : cal(C) arrow.r
    cal(I)$ is surjective in the tensor-category sense #cite(label("EGNO2015")) Definition 1.8.18,
    Proposition 1.8.19, and Section 6.3. Incompressibility makes this corestriction fully faithful.
    The injective and surjective Frobenius–Perron-dimension inequalities for finite tensor categories give,
    respectively,
  $ "FPdim" \( cal(C) \) lt.eq "FPdim" \( cal(I) \) \, wide "FPdim" \( cal(C) \) gt.eq "FPdim" \( cal(I) \) . $
  Equality in either inequality makes the corestriction an equivalence #cite(label("EGNO2015")) Propositions 6.3.3
    and 6.3.4.
  Corollary 4.61 of #cite(label("BEO2023")) now restricts $cal(I)$ to the full and plus higher Verlinde
    subcategories at heights $m lt.eq n$. For odd $p$, the bottom cases are included using the explicit convention
    $"Ver"_1 = "sVec"_k$ and $"Ver"_1^(+) = "Vec"_k$ of #cite(label("CoulembierEtingofOstrikIncompressible"))
    Section 1.3. With the low-level identifications above, these are exactly the displayed possibilities.
  Conversely, the full and plus categories in the list are incompressible for symmetric tensor functors by
    #cite(label("BEO2023")) Theorem 4.71 and Remark 4.72(iii).
    For $"Vec"_k$, fullness follows from $"End" \( upright(bold(1)) \) = k$.
    For $"sVec"_k$ in odd characteristic, a symmetric tensor functor sends the odd invertible generator to an odd
    invertible object. It cannot be isomorphic to the unit, since their self-symmetries are respectively $-
    1$ and $1$. The functor is therefore fully faithful on the two simple objects,
    and hence on all their finite direct sums. ◻
]

Every finite tensor category has moderate growth: multiplication by the class of a fixed object on the finite-rank
  Grothendieck group is a finite nonnegative matrix, which bounds the lengths of its tensor powers exponentially.
  Thus the corollary is the finite case of #cite(label("CoulembierEtingofOstrikIncompressible")) Conjecture B.

=== 8.2 Uniqueness in characteristic two <uniqueness-in-characteristic-two>

For $p = 2$, set

$ "Ver"_(2^oo) \( k \) := union.big_(n gt.eq 1) "Ver"_(2^n) \( k \) \, $

using the exact symmetric tower embeddings. A fiber functor to this union means a $k$-linear exact faithful strong
  symmetric monoidal functor, just as for a finite target.

#thm([Corollary 8.2.])[
  (Uniqueness into the characteristic-two union).  #emph[Let $k$ be algebraically closed of characteristic two and
    let $cal(C)$ be a finite symmetric tensor category over $k$. There is exactly one fiber functor
$ cal(C) arrow.r "Ver"_(2^oo) \( k \) $

 up to monoidal natural isomorphism. Its isomorphism class contains a representative that factors through
   $"Ver"_(2^n) \( k \)$ for some finite $n gt.eq 1$. For each fixed $n gt.eq 1$,
   there is at most one fiber functor $cal(C) arrow.r "Ver"_(2^n) \( k \)$ up to monoidal natural isomorphism.]
] <cor:characteristic-two-uniqueness>

#proof[
  Theorem 1 supplies a fiber functor to a finite stage, and composition with the tower inclusion supplies a fiber
    functor to the union. By #cite(label("CoulembierEtingofOstrikIncompressible")) Theorem 9.4.1,
    an arbitrary tensor category in characteristic two has at most one $k$-linear exact symmetric tensor functor
    to $"Ver"_(2^oo) \( k \)$ up to monoidal natural isomorphism.
    Applying this to $cal(C)$ proves uniqueness in the union.
    For two fiber functors to the same finite stage, this isomorphism lifts through the fully faithful tower
    inclusion; its naturality and tensor compatibility hold at that stage by faithfulness.
    This proves the fixed-stage assertion as well. ◻
]

=== 8.3 Generalized Tannaka reconstruction <generalized-tannaka-reconstruction>

Let $cal(V)$ be a finite symmetric tensor category over $k$.
  An affine group scheme internal to $cal(V)$ is specified by a commutative Hopf algebra $cal(O) \( G \)$ in
  $"Ind" \( cal(V) \)$. The fundamental group

$ pi \( cal(V) \) := underline(upright("Aut"))^⊗ \( "Id"_(cal(V)) \) $

acts canonically on every object of $"Ind" \( cal(V) \)$.
  Following #cite(label("CoulembierEtingofOstrikIncompressible")) Sections 4.1.1–4.1.4,
  a #emph[$cal(V)$-group] is an internal affine group scheme $G$ with a homomorphism $phi.alt :
  pi \( cal(V) \) arrow.r G$ such that the coaction on $cal(O) \( G \)$ induced by conjugation through $phi.alt$
  equals its canonical $pi \( cal(V) \)$-coaction. The category $"Rep" \( G \, phi.alt \)$ consists of
  $G$-representations in $cal(V)$ whose restriction along $phi.alt$ is the canonical $pi \( cal(V) \)$-action.
  Write $omega : "Rep" \( G \, phi.alt \) arrow.r cal(V)$ for the forgetful functor.

#thm([Corollary 8.3.])[
  (Reconstruction over a finite higher Verlinde level).  #emph[Let $k$ be algebraically closed of characteristic
    $p > 0$, and let $cal(C)$ be a finite symmetric tensor category over $k$.
    Choose $n gt.eq 1$ and a fiber functor $F : cal(C) arrow.r cal(V) = "Ver"_(p^n) \( k \)$ as in Theorem 1.
    There is a $cal(V)$-group $\( G \, phi.alt \)$ and a $k$-linear symmetric tensor equivalence
$ E : cal(C) arrow.r^tilde.op "Rep" \( G \, phi.alt \) $

 with a monoidal natural isomorphism $omega compose E tilde.equiv F$. One may take
$ G = underline(upright("Aut"))^⊗ \( F \) \, wide phi.alt : pi \( cal(V) \) arrow.r
  underline(upright("Aut"))^⊗ \( F \) $

 to be the canonical homomorphism induced by $F$.]
] <cor:generalized-tannaka>

#proof[
  The finite-category conventions, including the simple unit supplied by Lemma 2,
    make $cal(C)$ and $cal(V)$ pretannakian categories in the terminology of
    #cite(label("CoulembierEtingofOstrikIncompressible")). The chosen $F$ is a tensor functor in that paper’s
    sense. Its Theorem 4.2.1 assigns to $F$ the $cal(V)$-group displayed above,
    and the inverse assignment is the forgetful functor from $"Rep" \( G \, phi.alt \)$.
    The resulting equivalence over $cal(V)$ is precisely the one stated here. ◻
]

The classification and existence assertions here remain confined to finite categories.
  Uniqueness is asserted in characteristic two, both in the tower union and, whenever a fiber functor exists,
  at each fixed finite stage. The existence theorem supplies some finite height depending on $cal(C)$;
  it does not supply existence at every prescribed height. In the reconstruction, $G$ is asserted to be affine;
  no finiteness or finite-type property of this group scheme is used or claimed.

